To say Tesla has changed the auto industry is no exaggeration.

What started as a niche EV brand quickly became a symbol of modern driving. With sleek designs, zero-emission performance, and high-end features like ‘Autopilot’, Tesla promised a smarter, smoother future on the road. And thousands of Californians bought in.

But owning a Tesla doesn’t always feel futuristic.

Drivers frequently report recurring software bugs, phantom braking, range drops, battery issues, and touchscreen failures. When service visits don’t fix the problem, many are left wondering what to do next.

Fortunately, California lemon law applies to Teslas just like any other vehicle sold or leased with a manufacturer’s warranty. However, these cases present unique challenges. Over-the-air updates, a lack of dealership infrastructure, and Tesla’s push for arbitration make the process more complicated than it should be.

