In the sections below, we’ll debunk the most common myths around Tesla lemon law, and explain why you should work with a qualified California lemon law attorney.

## Myth #1: Teslas Aren’t Covered Under Lemon Laws

This is the most common misconception, and it’s flat-out wrong.

[California’s lemon law](/info/how-it-works), also known as the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act, applies to all consumer vehicles sold or leased with a manufacturer’s warranty in the state - including Teslas.

Suppose your Tesla has a defect that affects its use, value, or safety, and the automaker can’t fix it within a reasonable number of repair attempts. In that case, you may be entitled to a refund or replacement.

The law doesn’t care whether your car runs on gas, electricity, or fairy dust. What matters is that it came with a warranty and isn’t functioning as it should.

Keep in mind: Due to [recent law changes](https://calmatters.digitaldemocracy.org/bills/ca_202320240ab1755?slug=CA_202320240AB1755), California lemon law claims must now be filed within one year after the warranty expires and no later than six years from the car’s original delivery date. Additionally, protections are typically limited to new, leased, and manufacturer-certified pre-owned (CPO) vehicles sold with a new warranty.

Tesla lemon law cases are very real, and lemon law lawyers across California have been helping owners file and win these claims for years.

## Myth #2: Software Glitches Don’t Count

A big part of Tesla’s appeal is its software-driven design. Unfortunately, it’s also a big part of the problem. Many owners run into tech-related issues that traditional manufacturers don’t have.

Here are just a few examples of defects that may qualify under Tesla lemon law:

- Autopilot or Full Self-Driving (FSD) errors

- Charging problems or battery degradation

- Software updates that introduce new bugs or create malfunctions

- Screens that freeze, reboot, or go black while driving

- Doors that won’t open or handles that fail

- Sudden drops in driving range

These glitches typically affect how the car operates, which is one of the main qualifiers under CA lemon law.

A [2022 J.D. Power study](https://www.jdpower.com/business/press-releases/2022-us-initial-quality-study-iqs) found that Tesla owners report far more software-related issues than the industry average. Driver-assist tech and touchscreen responsiveness were two of the most common complaints, underscoring just how often the core systems go haywire.

If you’ve experienced recurring software problems, Tesla’s usual response may be to “patch” the issue with a new update. But if there’s no permanent fix after a reasonable number of attempts, or if the defects put you in danger on the road, they can absolutely count toward a potential lemon law claim.

## Myth #3: Tesla’s Arbitration Process Is the Only Option

Tesla likes to direct unhappy customers to its internal arbitration process. While it may seem like the quickest way out of a messy situation, it’s not legally required and is rarely in your best interest.

You do not have to go through arbitration to obtain help under CA lemon law. In fact, doing so can limit your ability to pursue stronger remedies through a civil claim. Arbitration is handled by a third-party entity that works with Tesla regularly. The company has every incentive to resolve the claim as cheaply and quietly as possible (in Tesla’s favor).

A qualified [Tesla lemon law lawyer](/) will almost always recommend filing a formal claim. This puts the pressure back on Tesla, opens the door to a full refund or replacement, and makes sure your legal team can fight for every dollar you’re owed, including things like registration fees, loan interest, and legal expenses.

Important: Tesla has a forced arbitration clause in the purchase order. When you buy a Tesla, you have 30 days to opt out. Do not forget to do this.

## Myth #4: You Need to Have a Major Breakdown

Another common misconception is that your car must completely fall apart before it qualifies as a lemon. Again, that’s not true.

Under California law, a defect doesn’t have to be catastrophic. It just has to substantially impair the use, value, or safety of the vehicle.

Even if your Tesla vehicle still drives, ask yourself:

- Does this issue affect how I use the car day-to-day?

- Has this issue made me feel unsafe?

- Have I taken the car in multiple times (typically two or more for a safety-related defect) and still have nothing to show for it?

If the answer is yes to any of those questions, you may be driving a lemon. The longer you wait, the harder it becomes to make your case - which brings us to our next point.

## Myth #5: You Can File a Claim Anytime

This is a dangerous misconception. California lemon law claims must be filed within one year after the manufacturer’s warranty expiration date and no later than six years from the vehicle’s original delivery date.

That’s a much shorter window than many Tesla owners realize. Waiting too long, or continuing to drive the car for thousands of miles without attempting repairs, can weaken your case or even disqualify you completely.

